[{:db/id 17592186129267 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-29c4348e-6b58-58ee-9f24-8f11cdb0cbe1" "question-34d0810f-9c28-578b-861d-9088f229fffb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129268 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fa2c3789-4a9d-5deb-a378-eba9cd152cf2" "question-f86783a2-30c3-5d18-9eb0-dc2efdbbae6a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129278 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ae4823a-479e-5870-b42a-306885da9109" "question-8382ccc3-43fb-56df-8da1-731b85bcec1a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-29c4348e-6b58-58ee-9f24-8f11cdb0cbe1" :question/id #uuid "29c4348e-6b58-58ee-9f24-8f11cdb0cbe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161613" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8d/8dc4ea0ad2bb776fb31132e1ff8d97efdd2c166dc23ffbc8641f8d7034b8d487.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6d846f8-2c72-5e60-9280-2f6e5faaefd4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6d846f8-2c72-5e60-9280-2f6e5faaefd4" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d846f8-2c72-5e60-9280-2f6e5faaefd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2fa8d886-4e3c-5e48-b553-b40bcf5fb8a1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-880a8f8a-f3c5-58bb-8d3c-3a0e61a860b8" "answer-dcf0414c-80b1-5d90-b7d4-18b04ab2c214" "answer-5ff27779-26b4-5f50-86c1-895b48b634f5" "answer-2fa8d886-4e3c-5e48-b553-b40bcf5fb8a1" "answer-845094cf-59d5-5624-ae32-03926b0d10f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-880a8f8a-f3c5-58bb-8d3c-3a0e61a860b8" :answer/id #uuid "880a8f8a-f3c5-58bb-8d3c-3a0e61a860b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-r}}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcf0414c-80b1-5d90-b7d4-18b04ab2c214" :answer/id #uuid "dcf0414c-80b1-5d90-b7d4-18b04ab2c214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ff27779-26b4-5f50-86c1-895b48b634f5" :answer/id #uuid "5ff27779-26b4-5f50-86c1-895b48b634f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-r}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fa8d886-4e3c-5e48-b553-b40bcf5fb8a1" :answer/id #uuid "2fa8d886-4e3c-5e48-b553-b40bcf5fb8a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-845094cf-59d5-5624-ae32-03926b0d10f6" :answer/id #uuid "845094cf-59d5-5624-ae32-03926b0d10f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-34d0810f-9c28-578b-861d-9088f229fffb" :question/id #uuid "34d0810f-9c28-578b-861d-9088f229fffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161604" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ between the surfaces $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $z=3.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-587e6da7-3775-5bfa-a95f-b626a3ad5c01"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-587e6da7-3775-5bfa-a95f-b626a3ad5c01" :answer-field/id #uuid "587e6da7-3775-5bfa-a95f-b626a3ad5c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-841fab14-6f1b-500a-ab2e-cd3ad15098fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-48587302-f52a-53a7-9f31-47825d8a1075" "answer-841fab14-6f1b-500a-ab2e-cd3ad15098fe" "answer-a75b7c7c-ec28-5349-b0da-7fdd1b563361" "answer-b90b5f49-c248-5418-886a-73d93a5226bf" "answer-90b8d8fe-ee7b-5218-86d0-ad24858cf4c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48587302-f52a-53a7-9f31-47825d8a1075" :answer/id #uuid "48587302-f52a-53a7-9f31-47825d8a1075" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r}^{3}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-841fab14-6f1b-500a-ab2e-cd3ad15098fe" :answer/id #uuid "841fab14-6f1b-500a-ab2e-cd3ad15098fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a75b7c7c-ec28-5349-b0da-7fdd1b563361" :answer/id #uuid "a75b7c7c-ec28-5349-b0da-7fdd1b563361" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{r}^{2}}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b90b5f49-c248-5418-886a-73d93a5226bf" :answer/id #uuid "b90b5f49-c248-5418-886a-73d93a5226bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{r}^{3}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90b8d8fe-ee7b-5218-86d0-ad24858cf4c8" :answer/id #uuid "90b8d8fe-ee7b-5218-86d0-ad24858cf4c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{{r}^{2}}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa2c3789-4a9d-5deb-a378-eba9cd152cf2" :question/id #uuid "fa2c3789-4a9d-5deb-a378-eba9cd152cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161637" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b6/b66b8db18b2f5773addc2d00c02f2f15f4a7ead53323418e63bd50457f74466b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-5\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae592001-7c80-5683-9d64-6ebfa88e4b3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae592001-7c80-5683-9d64-6ebfa88e4b3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae592001-7c80-5683-9d64-6ebfa88e4b3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4e25a86-fc0b-500c-9ea6-617a19745775" "answer-88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" "answer-aa5a0734-d3ec-5220-8c59-ae2d980b4839" "answer-f1e27cb0-5d40-54cb-b1f4-f82c347f9fc7" "answer-6caf10ce-b939-51d0-8ac6-b9727918700b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4e25a86-fc0b-500c-9ea6-617a19745775" :answer/id #uuid "d4e25a86-fc0b-500c-9ea6-617a19745775" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" :answer/id #uuid "88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa5a0734-d3ec-5220-8c59-ae2d980b4839" :answer/id #uuid "aa5a0734-d3ec-5220-8c59-ae2d980b4839" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1e27cb0-5d40-54cb-b1f4-f82c347f9fc7" :answer/id #uuid "f1e27cb0-5d40-54cb-b1f4-f82c347f9fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6caf10ce-b939-51d0-8ac6-b9727918700b" :answer/id #uuid "6caf10ce-b939-51d0-8ac6-b9727918700b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f86783a2-30c3-5d18-9eb0-dc2efdbbae6a" :question/id #uuid "f86783a2-30c3-5d18-9eb0-dc2efdbbae6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161633" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\le 0,\\,-2\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/83/831de8cf1d9833f8674a7e4cb69d01098970eefb9cf576c0537fa7be471c8c2c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-2\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a4c28d8-7673-5151-abc1-64dbe75c6bbb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a4c28d8-7673-5151-abc1-64dbe75c6bbb" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4c28d8-7673-5151-abc1-64dbe75c6bbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa6ce7d9-8ba5-51ed-8961-057d4fc31ae0" "answer-b00512c6-580b-5ace-be32-2aca9c459007" "answer-c7c92edf-23c0-5c0c-b7bb-0f570aaf2732" "answer-07316687-6f83-5fed-b3ec-6bddf779b76b" "answer-8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa6ce7d9-8ba5-51ed-8961-057d4fc31ae0" :answer/id #uuid "aa6ce7d9-8ba5-51ed-8961-057d4fc31ae0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b00512c6-580b-5ace-be32-2aca9c459007" :answer/id #uuid "b00512c6-580b-5ace-be32-2aca9c459007" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7c92edf-23c0-5c0c-b7bb-0f570aaf2732" :answer/id #uuid "c7c92edf-23c0-5c0c-b7bb-0f570aaf2732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07316687-6f83-5fed-b3ec-6bddf779b76b" :answer/id #uuid "07316687-6f83-5fed-b3ec-6bddf779b76b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060" :answer/id #uuid "8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ae4823a-479e-5870-b42a-306885da9109" :question/id #uuid "8ae4823a-479e-5870-b42a-306885da9109" :question/math-academy-id "q-161608" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=4-x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/62/62abd56d2d374afb7c608aa64f701cfe94f096b823f964697a929c7c24f3a073.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4-x=4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}(4-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}-{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[{r}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\theta -\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{5}(0-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2673f4f3-d83e-5c77-94fc-178bcf193249"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2673f4f3-d83e-5c77-94fc-178bcf193249" :answer-field/id #uuid "2673f4f3-d83e-5c77-94fc-178bcf193249" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f8e86634-3513-5357-bbd9-f8582d0d5b2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8e86634-3513-5357-bbd9-f8582d0d5b2d" "answer-f2487b1b-ed8a-59bd-8da7-a1715766603f" "answer-230d0c4b-7bc5-5af6-8cf4-b180ffe873c2" "answer-584cb703-7f97-5df9-a64a-8ecc7abca1af" "answer-27240ef3-56dd-5f35-80d3-a273aa752805"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8e86634-3513-5357-bbd9-f8582d0d5b2d" :answer/id #uuid "f8e86634-3513-5357-bbd9-f8582d0d5b2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2487b1b-ed8a-59bd-8da7-a1715766603f" :answer/id #uuid "f2487b1b-ed8a-59bd-8da7-a1715766603f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-230d0c4b-7bc5-5af6-8cf4-b180ffe873c2" :answer/id #uuid "230d0c4b-7bc5-5af6-8cf4-b180ffe873c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-584cb703-7f97-5df9-a64a-8ecc7abca1af" :answer/id #uuid "584cb703-7f97-5df9-a64a-8ecc7abca1af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27240ef3-56dd-5f35-80d3-a273aa752805" :answer/id #uuid "27240ef3-56dd-5f35-80d3-a273aa752805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8382ccc3-43fb-56df-8da1-731b85bcec1a" :question/id #uuid "8382ccc3-43fb-56df-8da1-731b85bcec1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161621" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ for $x\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=9+x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/e2/e289cfbd210112a619018a7b905f7f2b1a9859ecb84b2517dd745705624d79d7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+9=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )[z]_{z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}9{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[3{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\sqrt{3})^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}9\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{3}(1-(-1))+9\\sqrt{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-13092669-e4dd-5fb1-923f-23241a6ac139"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-13092669-e4dd-5fb1-923f-23241a6ac139" :answer-field/id #uuid "13092669-e4dd-5fb1-923f-23241a6ac139" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0d3c3ffc-8f92-544d-a992-79894282f2ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d3c3ffc-8f92-544d-a992-79894282f2ca" "answer-f1a72efe-970d-57a6-bbba-8a4a895dd196" "answer-76bee58e-203b-522c-96c7-8110717e932b" "answer-fde9bf40-8671-597c-957b-0c2763e035e1" "answer-68ea0354-f2c8-53b4-a86e-b9c1d0183a0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d3c3ffc-8f92-544d-a992-79894282f2ca" :answer/id #uuid "0d3c3ffc-8f92-544d-a992-79894282f2ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1a72efe-970d-57a6-bbba-8a4a895dd196" :answer/id #uuid "f1a72efe-970d-57a6-bbba-8a4a895dd196" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76bee58e-203b-522c-96c7-8110717e932b" :answer/id #uuid "76bee58e-203b-522c-96c7-8110717e932b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fde9bf40-8671-597c-957b-0c2763e035e1" :answer/id #uuid "fde9bf40-8671-597c-957b-0c2763e035e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68ea0354-f2c8-53b4-a86e-b9c1d0183a0a" :answer/id #uuid "68ea0354-f2c8-53b4-a86e-b9c1d0183a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130456 :tutorial/content "We can evaluate triple integrals by changing the variables to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the finite region enclosed between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a63e349d2776fe07077379b9d603e32ad2ff264cfa8005a226422b44f7e100fe.png)\n\n \n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nRecall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b2/b2f8a6fe7f21b062eec294522e411c1e471b564eae68763d4767f917d65b54b0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $R$ is\n\n- bounded below by $z = 0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 = 1 - r^2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-r^2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dae76df462a695e655ffdde01559d19bf245aa6d601100fb8c7bc1024106a4c8.png)\n\n \n\nTo express our integral in terms of cylindrical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, {\\color{blue}\\mathrm{d}V} = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in cylindrical polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}V}} = {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of ${\\color{blue}{r}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1}} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1 - r^2 }} \\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black} r^3} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n=\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130470 :tutorial/content "Let's now build some intuition behind the meaning of the volume element $\\textrm d V.$ This will help us to understand the change of variables formula in cylindrical polar coordinates. We start by considering the usual rectangular coordinate system.\n\nSuppose we have a point $P$ and a neighboring point $Q$ in the Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below. \n\n![](images/25/2535744585beb7fed89d760c0290a04f54d0a08c8f7e564b17c8e42432da031e.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d x$ parallel to the $x$-axis so that the new point $P'$ has the same $x$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we move a distance $\\textrm d y$ parallel to the $y$-axis so that the new point $P''$ has the same $y$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ parallel to the $z$-axis to land to $Q.$\n\nThese movements span the rectangular solid shown above. The volume of this solid, denoted $\\textrm d V,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}z.\n$$\n\nThus, the element $\\textrm d V$ represents the volume generated by moving from the point $P$ to the point $Q$ in this coordinate system." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130473 :tutorial/content "Let's now repeat this process for a cylindrical polar system. Consider the points $P$ and $Q$ shown below.\n\n![](images/24/243d720546357a33eac3f96b84755cf6aeebafdef3a3672a3d29989147f134c2.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d r$ so that the new point $P'$ has the same $r$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we sweep through an angle $\\textrm d \\theta$ so that the new point $P''$ has the same $\\theta$-coordinate as $Q.$ The arc length traced out by this movement approximately equals $r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ vertically to land on $Q.$\n\nThese three movements trace out a solid that's approximately rectangular, and so the approximate volume of this solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}r \\cdot r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\mathrm{d}z=  r\\,\\mathrm{d}z\\,\\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129389 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
